{
  "name": "Dune Hopper",
  "description": "A dune sea at noon, and the first trail where distance matters: hop from crest to crest. Real dunes have a long, gentle slope on the side the wind comes from, a sharp crest, and a steep slip face beyond, so every crest throws you forward onto a downhill landing. First the edge of the sand: stony flats and two single dunes to ride over, with an apple past the first crest. Then the doubles: two dunes close together. Carry speed from the first crest over the hollow onto the second dune's slip face; too slow and you land in the hollow, which only costs time. The apples float over the hollows, so a clean double takes them. The ripples: small waves of sand to skim or take crest to crest, with an apple low in a hollow. The buried ruin: a stone tower half swallowed by the dunes. Its apple waits deep in the hollow beside the tower, so here the skill is the other way round: ease off and land short, or you fly over it. Last, the great dune: a long climb that needs the speed you bring, with the flower floating over its crest and a rock outcrop beyond. Every miss lands on sand: nothing here ends the run. Intended apple order 1 to 6.",
  "lesson": "Distance control: hopping from crest to crest. Carry the right speed to land on the next dune, not in the hollow.",
  "alsoNew": "Sun glare, cacti, a dune sea and a buried ruin.",
  "world": "The Desert",
